      <p>The calculator iterates year 1 through the maximum of your analysis period or 20 years to find the first year where build cumulative cost falls at or below buy cumulative cost. It then interpolates between that year and the previous year to give you the break-even in months, not just whole years.</p>
      <p>When growth rates are zero, both formulas simplify to flat annual cost multiplied by years. The geometric series form handles the compounding case where g &gt; 0 or r &gt; 0.</p>
      <h2 id="when-to-build">When building makes sense</h2>
      <p>Build wins when the subscription cost is high relative to build complexity, when your requirements are genuinely unique, or when the product is so central to your business that you can't afford vendor dependency. Here are the clearest signals that building is the right call.</p>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Why build wins</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>No SaaS covers &gt;80% of your requirements</td><td>Customization cost on a 60% fit often exceeds build cost within 2 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Subscription price exceeds build + maintenance at year 5</td><td>The cumulative math favors build; break-even is reachable</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tool is a core competitive advantage</td><td>Letting a vendor own your core capability creates strategic risk</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deep integration with proprietary data or systems</td><td>API-based integrations with SaaS tools add cost and latency</td></tr>
            <tr><td>High user volume with per-seat pricing</td><td>Per-seat SaaS costs scale linearly; build costs do not</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Regulated industry with strict data residency requirements</td><td>Not all SaaS vendors meet HIPAA, SOC2, or GDPR requirements</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The team size and subscription model are the two biggest factors. If a SaaS tool charges per seat and you have 500 users at $20/seat/month ($120K/year), you can often build a focused internal tool for $150K and be in cost parity by year 2 - even with maintenance included. That math obviously flips if you only have 20 users.</p>
      <h2 id="when-to-buy">When buying makes sense</h2>
      <p>Buy wins when speed-to-value matters, when your engineering team is best deployed on core product, or when the problem being solved is genuinely commodity functionality.</p>
      <p>The majority of build vs. buy analyses should end in a buy decision - most internal software needs are not unique enough to justify the sustained maintenance commitment.</p>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Why buy wins</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Tool needed in weeks, not months</td><td>SaaS deploys immediately; build takes 3-18 months before first useful version</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Low user count with flat per-seat pricing</td><td>Subscription cost stays predictable; build maintenance does not</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Category is evolving rapidly (AI, analytics)</td><td>SaaS vendor absorbs R&amp;D cost; you get new features automatically</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Engineering team is at capacity on core product</td><td>Opportunity cost of diverting engineers exceeds subscription cost</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Well-defined problem with mature SaaS solutions</td><td>CRM, HR, accounting, email - these are solved problems</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Analysis period is under 3 years</td><td>Build almost never recoups initial investment in under 3 years</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>When I've reviewed software spend at my previous employer where engineering was the bottleneck, buying almost always won on the full model - even where build looked cheaper on a subscription vs. development cost comparison.</p>
      <p>The factor that consistently tipped the analysis was the cost of one senior engineer's time for 3-6 months, which is frequently $60-$120K in salary alone, before the ongoing maintenance obligation.</p>
      <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes in build vs. buy analyses</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Forgetting ongoing maintenance:</strong> The single most common error. Industry benchmarks put annual maintenance at 15-25% of initial development cost. Missing this single line item routinely makes build appear 2-3x cheaper than reality over a 5-year horizon.</li>
        <li><strong>Using a 1-year analysis window:</strong> Build almost never wins at year one. Using a 1-year comparison biases every analysis toward buy, regardless of long-term economics. Use a 5-7 year window to capture the period where build costs flatten and subscription costs compound.</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring subscription price escalation:</strong> 73% of SaaS vendors raised prices in 2023, with average software price inflation of 8.7% - more than double general CPI (Vertice, 2023). A $24K/year subscription at 8% annual growth costs $43K in year 10 - a 79% increase. Not modeling this understates the buy cost significantly.</li>
        <li><strong>Counting only developer time for initial build:</strong> Project management, QA, design, documentation, and stakeholder reviews typically add 30-50% to raw development time. A project estimated at 3 engineer-months often takes 5 calendar months when total effort is counted.</li>
        <li><strong>Excluding integration and onboarding costs for buy:</strong> Implementation fees for enterprise SaaS tools often equal or exceed the first year's subscription. A $24K/year tool with a $20K implementation fee has a true first-year cost of $44K - often missed when comparing to build.</li>
        <li><strong>Not modeling the switching cost:</strong> Build analyses typically don't account for the cost to exit a SaaS vendor in year 3 or 5 if the tool doesn't meet needs. Data migration, retraining, and integration rebuild can cost more than a year's subscription.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions about build vs. buy</h2>
      <h3>When should you build instead of buy?</h3>
      <p>Build when your requirements are genuinely unique and no off-the-shelf product covers more than 70-80% of them, when you need deep integration with proprietary systems, or when the subscription cost over 5+ years clearly exceeds build plus maintenance. Build also makes sense when the product is core to your competitive advantage and you can't afford vendor lock-in.</p>
      <h3>What hidden costs do most build vs. buy analyses miss?</h3>
      <p>The two most commonly missed costs are ongoing software maintenance (typically 15-25% of initial development cost per year) and the <a href="/glossary/opportunity-cost">opportunity cost</a> of developer time. When your engineering team builds internal tools, they're not building revenue-generating product. This opportunity cost is rarely included in build analyses but is often the largest single factor when total cost is calculated honestly.</p>
      <h3>What is the break-even point in a build vs. buy decision?</h3>
      <p>The break-even point is the month at which cumulative build costs (initial investment plus all maintenance and hosting) first equal cumulative buy costs (setup fee plus all subscription payments). After that point, building is cheaper on a cumulative basis. This calculator interpolates between years to give you the break-even in months, not just whole years.</p>
      <h3>Is building software always cheaper in the long run?</h3>
      <p>Not always. Build costs continue to grow as software requires maintenance, security patches, and infrastructure. If subscription prices stay flat or the vendor improves the product at their cost, the subscription can remain cheaper indefinitely. Build only wins the long-run cost race when maintenance stays modest relative to subscription savings - typically under 20% of initial cost per year.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between build vs. buy and make vs. buy?</h3>
      <p>Make vs. buy is the broader strategic framework used in manufacturing and supply chain management - should a company produce a component internally or purchase it from a supplier? Build vs. buy is the software-specific application of the same principle. The underlying economics are identical; the terminology differs by industry.</p>
